import Foundation

enum ERPStudioHelp {
    static let groupDuration = HelpTopic(title: "Group recording duration", summary: "Each subject has its own seed, intervals, and response variability. This option extends each recording as needed to include its actual response tails; it never shortens the base duration.", guidance: "Subjects can have different recording lengths. Turn this off to require the same base duration for all subjects; generation will fail if any subject's responses do not fit.")
    static let groupEffects = HelpTopic(title: "Multi-condition group effects", summary: "Choose a reference condition whose nominal waveform stays fixed across subjects. A subject's ERP-effect multiplier scales every other condition's waveform difference from that reference. The same multiplier is used for all differences, so their between-subject variation is correlated.", guidance: "Default comparisons report each other condition minus the reference. Add named pairwise comparisons here, or weighted contrasts with the CLI. Truth contains exact Gaussian waveform terms, not a sum of peak amplitudes. These are nominal effects before recipes, omissions, peak occurrence, jitter, noise, and projection; observed condition averages can differ. ERP-effect SD is a fraction of the nominal difference; zero preserves the population waveforms, and negative drawn multipliers can reverse the effects.")
    static let legacy = HelpTopic(title: "Existing ERP scenarios", summary: "The editor supports Gaussian peaks sharing one ERP source. Opening a legacy single-Gaussian scenario copies its frequent and target waveforms into independent conditions. Placed-source, biphasic, measured-template, or nonzero skew/correlation scenarios keep their original generation path.", guidance: "The editor uses exact recipe/omission counts and uniformly varied intervals. Its random draws differ from legacy scenarios. To start a new editable design from a different legacy model, turn ERPs off and on.")
    static let conditions = HelpTopic(title: "Conditions", summary: "Each condition has its own name, event code, trial share, waveform, and recipe mix. Add as many conditions as your experiment needs; one condition is also supported.", guidance: "Add or duplicate gives the new condition 20% and proportionally reduces existing shares. Remove redistributes the remaining shares to 100%. Selecting a condition changes the waveform being edited.")
    static let conditionName = HelpTopic(title: "Condition name", summary: "A unique, readable name written as the stimulus event label in the MFF. The internal condition ID stays unchanged when you rename it, keeping truth and scores linked.")
    static let eventCode = HelpTopic(title: "Event code", summary: "The stimulus marker used for epoching this condition. Use 1–4 ASCII letters or digits, unique across conditions and distinct from built-in artifact markers such as TREV, QRSd, and blnk.")
    static let share = HelpTopic(title: "Trial share", summary: "Percentage of all scheduled trials assigned to this condition, before response omissions. Shares must total 100%.", guidance: "Counts use largest-remainder rounding so their sum is exactly the requested trial count. A small or zero share can yield zero trials. Normalize shares rescales the entered proportions to 100%; it does not change their relative ratios.")
    static let waveform = HelpTopic(title: "Waveform editor", summary: "Double-click inside the plot to add a Gaussian peak. Drag a handle horizontally to move its latency and vertically to change the combined waveform at that time. The inspector edits the selected component's own amplitude and width.", guidance: "Peaks add together; close peaks can merge, so a handle need not be a local maximum. Negative amplitudes create negative deflections. All peaks in this editor share one source/topography. Values are the peak-normalized ERP contribution before recording re-reference, not a promise about every sensor. Positive is plotted upward.")
    static let latency = HelpTopic(title: "Peak latency", summary: "Time in milliseconds after the stimulus at which this Gaussian component reaches its peak, before trial jitter. Drag left/right or type a positive value. Trial latencies are clipped at 5 ms when jitter would put them earlier.")
    static let amplitude = HelpTopic(title: "Component amplitude", summary: "Signed peak amplitude in microvolts before summing components and before trial variability. Negative values make a negative-going component. Overlapping components can make the combined waveform higher or lower than this value.")
    static let width = HelpTopic(title: "Peak width", summary: "Gaussian standard deviation σ in milliseconds. Larger widths make a broader component without changing its individual peak amplitude. Full width at half maximum is approximately 2.355 × σ.")
    static let occurrence = HelpTopic(title: "Peak occurrence", summary: "Percentage of non-omitted trials in this condition that contain the selected peak. Other peaks are unaffected. Assignments are independently shuffled per peak, so missing peaks can overlap across trials.", guidance: "The count is rounded to the nearest whole trial. This is separate from omission, which removes the entire ERP while retaining the stimulus and sensor noise.")
    static let jitter = HelpTopic(title: "Response latency jitter", summary: "Standard deviation of a zero-mean normal timing shift in milliseconds. All peaks within one trial receive the same shift, preserving their relative timing. Zero makes the response time-locked except for independent peak jitter or divergent recipes.")
    static let amplitudeJitter = HelpTopic(title: "Amplitude variation", summary: "Standard deviation as a percentage of the nominal amplitude. Every trial receives one common normal gain with mean 1; gains below zero are clipped to zero. A value of 20% uses a gain SD of 0.2. This variation does not invert polarity; the Inverted recipe does.")
    static let independent = HelpTopic(title: "Independent peak jitter", summary: "Additional normal latency jitter, in milliseconds SD, drawn separately for each peak and added to the whole-response shift. Use it when components should move relative to one another.")
    static let widthVariation = HelpTopic(title: "Width variation", summary: "Trial-to-trial standard deviation of a shared width multiplier, expressed as a percentage. Multipliers have mean 1 and are clipped at 0.05 to keep every Gaussian width positive. Divergent recipes apply an additional width multiplier.")
    static let noise = HelpTopic(title: "Added sensor noise", summary: "Continuous, independently drawn noise on every EEG channel in the noisy recording only. The setting is its theoretical RMS in microvolts before re-reference; finite-recording RMS can differ. Clean ground truth retains the ERP without this added noise.", options: [.init(name: "White", detail: "Independent Gaussian samples."), .init(name: "Smoothed", detail: "Gaussian AR(1) noise with a 20 ms time constant and the same marginal RMS.")], guidance: "This adds to the Background, Cardiac, Gradient, and other simulator settings. It is present on omitted trials too. The ERP preview includes this noise but excludes other artifacts, background EEG, spatial projection, and neighboring-trial overlap.")
    static let omissions = HelpTopic(title: "Omitted responses", summary: "Percentage of trials within each condition with no ERP at all. Events and continuous noise remain. Counts are rounded per condition, and recipes are allocated only among the remaining trials.")
    static let trials = HelpTopic(title: "Trial count", summary: "Total number of stimulus events across all conditions, including omitted responses. Condition percentages determine the actual counts; the counts next to each condition show the rounding result.")
    static let start = HelpTopic(title: "First stimulus", summary: "Time in seconds from the start of the recording to the first stimulus. Interval variation begins with the gap between the first and second events.")
    static let isi = HelpTopic(title: "Interstimulus interval", summary: "Time from one stimulus onset to the next. Each interval is drawn uniformly from mean ISI minus the ± range to mean ISI plus that range. The range must be smaller than the mean; zero gives fixed intervals.", guidance: "This changes event spacing, not response latency. Short intervals allow responses to overlap in the generated recording. Legacy scenarios instead jitter onsets around a fixed grid.")
    static let duration = HelpTopic(title: "Fit recording", summary: "Computes the actual seeded schedule and the end of every realized response (through six Gaussian standard deviations). Fit recording extends the recording to include all of them; it never shortens an existing recording.", guidance: "Recheck after changing the seed, ISI, number of trials, jitter, or widths. Generate is disabled if the schedule or waveform tails do not fit.")
    static let recipes = HelpTopic(title: "Trial recipes and classification", summary: "Assigns exactly one recipe to each non-omitted trial within the selected condition. Typical fills the remainder to 100%. Whole-response variability, peak occurrence, and noise still apply.", options: [.init(name: "Typical", detail: "Uses the condition's waveform with the configured variability."), .init(name: "Attenuated", detail: "Multiplies the entire ERP by the attenuation gain."), .init(name: "Inverted", detail: "Reverses the ERP polarity without reversing sensor noise."), .init(name: "Divergent", detail: "Applies an extra uniform latency shift and width multiplier.")], guidance: "These are generation recipes, not guaranteed TrialsView labels. TrialsView measures similarity to its reference average; noise, timing, and the mixture can change its classifications. Recipe and realized-peak truth are saved for comparison.")
    static let attenuation = HelpTopic(title: "Attenuated gain", summary: "Multiplier for the ERP in Attenuated trials. A gain of 0.3 makes every present peak 30% of its ordinary amplitude, in addition to global amplitude variation. Values range from 0 to 1.")
    static let divergence = HelpTopic(title: "Divergent recipe", summary: "Adds a uniform timing shift from minus to plus the specified millisecond range, and multiplies all widths by a uniform draw between the lower and upper bounds. These changes apply only to Divergent trials and combine with global jitter.")
    static let preview = HelpTopic(title: "Trial preview", summary: "Shows the selected condition's nominal template (dashed), mean of all its simulated trials (solid), and up to twelve example trials (faint). Omissions are included in the mean. All condition plots use identical time and voltage scales.", guidance: "Show other conditions displays the remaining conditions alongside the selection. This is a source-normalized, stimulus-relative preview using the same trial sampler as generation, with illustrative sensor noise. It omits other background/artifact settings, spatial projection, and overlapping neighboring responses. Changing the seed resamples trials; hiding a condition does not change the experiment.")
}
